+### Incremental Chunk Re-meshing
+- **Definition:** A technique to keep deformation fast and seamless by only re-meshing chunks affected by edits, rather than the entire world [S2, unofficial].
+- **Steps:** Divide the world into chunks. When a dig or build operation modifies the density field, mark only the affected chunks as 'dirty'. In the `LateUpdate` phase of the frame, iterate through dirty chunks and re-mesh them using Marching Cubes [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of GPU-accelerated Marching Cubes emerged as a response to the computational limits of CPU-based extraction for high-resolution volumes. Early implementations utilized OpenGL Geometry Shaders to generate triangles directly from vertex data, leveraging the GPU's parallel architecture [3769-marching-cubes-with-gaussian-deformation]. As compute shaders became available, more sophisticated memory management techniques were developed, such as atomic counters for dynamic buffer allocation [1211-primal-grid-marching-cubes, 4082-gpu-marching-cubes-with-atomic-counter-emission] and histogram pyramids for atomic-free counting [2317-gpu-marching-cubes-with-histogram-pyramid]. These methods are widely used in real-time visualization, terrain generation, and medical imaging where rapid SDF-to-mesh conversion is required [3803-gpu-marching-cubes-terrain-pipeline-with-indirect-rendering, 3818-gpu-marching-cubes-with-distance-extraction].
+GPU acceleration for Marching Cubes emerged as a response to the computational intensity of processing large volumetric datasets on CPUs. Early implementations utilized OpenGL fragment shaders for classification and vertex shaders for geometry generation, often relying on the "potential triangles" approach to avoid dynamic buffer management [S1, unofficial]. As compute shaders became available in DirectX 11 and OpenGL 4.3, more sophisticated pipelines were developed that allowed for direct memory access and atomic operations, enabling real-time extraction from high-resolution volumes [S1, S2, unofficial]. The field has since diversified into specialized techniques like histogram pyramids for contention-free rendering and capacity-safe pipelines for deterministic memory usage in applications like terrain generation and medical imaging [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
